{{!--
  Landing layout (GH-18, Figma 2696:54669 / 92452 / 94194 / 95558), selected
  with `:page-layout: home` on the page itself or `ui.default_layout` in the
  playbook.

  A shell only: every block on the page comes from the AsciiDoc document, so
  `page.contents` is dropped straight into the content column and the layout
  itself renders no landing content of its own. The blocks that have no
  AsciiDoc equivalent (card grids, feature tables, the CTA) arrive as
  Asciidoctor block extensions — see @inditextech/pdocs-asciidoc-extensions.

  What it shares with `default.hbs`: head, the header toolbar, the side menu,
  and the site scripts. What it drops: the page hero (hero.hbs), the "On this
  page" ToC and the `.doc` article surface, none of which the design's
  landing has, plus the page footer's previous/next and feedback rows — a
  landing is not a page in a reading sequence. `site-footer.hbs` takes their
  place, home-only for now.

  The two columns are items in the design system's own 12-column grid rather
  than a bespoke flex row, so their gutters and gaps match the header toolbar
  and every doc page above them. Their spans live in home.css, not in the
  grid's `--span-*` utility classes — see that file's own note on why.

  `data-page-home` is the side menu's pre-paint collapse hint (head-prelude.hbs,
  01-nav.ts): the landing starts with the menu collapsed unless the reader has
  already chosen otherwise. It is set from `page.home`, which Antora derives
  from the playbook's `site.start_page`, so a landing that is not the site's
  start page keeps the menu open.
--}}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en"{{#if page.home}} data-page-home{{/if}}>
  <head>
{{> head defaultPageTitle='Home'}}
  </head>
  <body class="home-page{{#with (or page.attributes.role page.role)}} {{{this}}}{{/with}}">
{{> header}}
    <div class="body">
{{> nav}}
      <main class="home ids-grid-layout ids-grid-layout--with-margin">
        {{!--
          Hero column: the product mark, name, labels and calls to action,
          plus an optional media panel — all of it authored on the landing
          document itself (GH-19). See home-hero.hbs for the attributes.
        --}}
        <aside class="home__hero">{{> home-hero}}</aside>
        {{!--
          The content column carries `doc` as well as `home__main`: a landing
          block's body is ordinary documentation content — paragraphs, lists,
          admonitions, code, tables, images — and it should be governed by the
          one stylesheet that already describes all of it (doc.css) rather than
          by a second, partial copy in home.css. What home.css keeps is only
          what the landing genuinely differs on: the column's own placement and
          the block heading's single dashed rule.
        --}}
        <div class="home__main doc">{{{page.contents}}}</div>
      </main>
    </div>
{{!--
  Outside `.body`, like the doc view's page footer (default.hbs): `.body` is a
  flex row at m and up (body.css) whose only item is `main`, so a footer nested
  in it would sit beside the content rather than below it. It yields to the side
  menu through its own `--side-menu-offset` margin instead — see site-footer.css.
--}}
{{> site-footer}}
{{> footer-scripts}}
  </body>
</html>
